    "body": "B2Gold President and CEO Mike Cinnamond at hte Mining Forum Americas last month in Colorado.\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby October 23, 2026\nB2Gold (TSX: BTO; NYSE-A: BTG) aims to turn its troubled Goose mine in Nunavut into a 300,000-oz.-a-year gold producer once crushing upgrades are complete, President and CEO Mike Cinnamond says.\nThe Vancouver-based miner is expanding an undersized crushing circuit that also suffered fire damage in April. It targets full processing capacity of 4,000 tonnes a day by mid-2027. Cinnamond maintained Goose’s production forecast of 170,000 to 200,000 oz. for this year, with higher throughput and stronger grades expected to support a bigger fourth quarter.\n“We’re flipping, we’re turning around, we’re starting to pull cash flow in,” Cinnamond told The Northern Miner late last month in Colorado Springs. “The repairs are a key test of B2Gold’s shift from heavy construction spending to generating more cash for shareholders.”\nNew ore supplies from Fekola Regional in Mali are expected to add production as they ramp up next year, while gold-price contracts that limit the company’s upside expire in January. Delivering those operational gains would give the miner more room to fund growth and share buybacks, with Cinnamond favouring opportunities within its portfolio as acquisitions elsewhere become more expensive.\nWatch below the full interview with The Miner’s Western Editor, Henry Lazenby:\nPrint\nTAG: Video\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Canada\nCOMPANY: B2Gold\nRepublish this article",
